&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;findClients&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;@/server/clients&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SORTABLE&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Set&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;city&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;status&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;owner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;balance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;lastInvoice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PAGE_SIZES&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;25&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;50&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;];&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;async&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;GET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;NextRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Partial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;DataTableQuery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{};&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;nextUrl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;searchParams&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;get&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;q&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;PAGE_SIZES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;25&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Math&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;floor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Number&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isArray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;sorting&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;((&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;SORTABLE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;has&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;));&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;desc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;desc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;undefined&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// findClients checks the session and maps each filter id to a known SQL clause&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;findClients&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;page&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;pageSize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;String&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;globalFilter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;""&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;filters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Array&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;isArray&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;columnFilters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;columnFilters&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Response&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;json&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Two server-mode details. The table has no faceted values without the full data, so a select filter lists only its &lt;code&gt;filterOptions&lt;/code&gt;, or the badge map's keys on a badge column: &lt;code&gt;status&lt;/code&gt; works as is, while &lt;code&gt;city&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;owner&lt;/code&gt; need an explicit &lt;code&gt;filterOptions&lt;/code&gt; list (a &lt;code&gt;select distinct&lt;/code&gt; on the server, for example). And quick filters need a &lt;code&gt;columnFilter&lt;/code&gt;, since a &lt;code&gt;filter&lt;/code&gt; function never reaches the server. If the page, sort and filters should live in the URL so a link reopens the same view, the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/nextjs-admin-dashboard-tutorial" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Next.js admin dashboard tutorial&lt;/a&gt; does that with &lt;code&gt;router.replace&lt;/code&gt; and a server component.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live demo: Data Table.&lt;/strong&gt; The original article embeds an interactive demo here (Sort, search, or select rows on page 1 and then on page 2 (long press a card on a phone): the query object the server receives is printed under the table, and the selection count keeps the rows from both pages). &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/tanstack-table-v9-data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Try it live on wingo-ui.com&lt;/a&gt; or open the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Data Table docs&lt;/a&gt;. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add data-table&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Server mode has a cost: every pause in typing is a request. When one request can load every row a user may see, client mode filters instantly and needs no query API. We switch past a few thousand rows, or when permissions decide which rows exist for a user.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should a data table look like on a phone?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A card per row. Eight columns on a 390px screen show about two at a time, and the header that names them scrolls out of sight. Below 768px the Data Table renders each row as a card, built from the same &lt;code&gt;mobile&lt;/code&gt; roles you saw in the columns:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;title&lt;/code&gt; is the card heading (by default the first column), &lt;code&gt;subtitle&lt;/code&gt; the line under it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;meta&lt;/code&gt; sits in the top corner (by default the first badge or the last number column).&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Up to four &lt;code&gt;field&lt;/code&gt; columns show as label and value pairs; &lt;code&gt;hidden&lt;/code&gt; keeps a column off the card.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Long press a card to start selecting, then tap to add more, and the bulk bar floats at the bottom of the screen. Sort, filters and the row menu open as bottom sheets, and client mode appends rows with Load more while server mode keeps a compact &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pagination" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pagination&lt;/a&gt; pager. The switch is a CSS breakpoint, so the server HTML already has the right layout before hydration and nothing jumps. &lt;code&gt;renderCard&lt;/code&gt; replaces the mapping with your own card, and &lt;code&gt;mobileLayout="scroll"&lt;/code&gt; keeps the table scrolling sideways for the rare grid that people compare column by column. The reasoning behind those defaults, with 44px touch targets and bottom sheets, is in &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/mobile-first-react-components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;mobile-first React components&lt;/a&gt;. How to pick the columns for a card, and the same layout on a plain shadcn/ui table, are in &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/responsive-table-mobile-cards" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;responsive tables on mobile&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For small read-only tables, such as invoice lines or a price list, the plain &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Table&lt;/a&gt; does the same card switch without TanStack, selection or a toolbar.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Should you build on TanStack directly or use a component?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Build directly when the table is one of a kind or when your design system already has the menus and sheets; use a component when you need the same admin table on twenty screens. The options we would consider, as of October 2026:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Option&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;License and price&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Server mode&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Bulk actions&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Pinning&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Phone layout&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;a href="https://tanstack.com/table/latest" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TanStack Table v9&lt;/a&gt; alone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MIT, free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;State only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;State only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;State only&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yours&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/docs/components/data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;shadcn data table guide&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MIT, free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Linked to TanStack docs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Selected-row count&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/sadmann7/tablecn" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tablecn&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;MIT, free&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, with Drizzle and PostgreSQL&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Action bar on selection&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Not listed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Not listed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Wingo UI Data Table&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Pro: $8 a month, $80 a year or $150 lifetime&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes (&lt;code&gt;mode="server"&lt;/code&gt;)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, floating on phones&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Yes, plus header menu&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cards below 768px&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;If you want Notion-style advanced filters over a Postgres table, tablecn is the better free starting point. If you only need sorting, one text filter and client pagination, the shadcn guide is enough. The Data Table, with the Table, Pagination and Toolbar it builds on, is part of &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Wingo UI Pro&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What should you build next?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open the playground on the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Data Table docs&lt;/a&gt;, switch &lt;code&gt;mode&lt;/code&gt; to server and watch the query object change as you sort and filter, then install it with &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add data-table&lt;/code&gt; after a one-time &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/docs/cli" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;CLI login&lt;/a&gt;. Other tables in the library, from the members table to the editable grid, are listed under &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/category/tables" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tables&lt;/a&gt;, and more component tutorials live in the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/category/components" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;component guides&lt;/a&gt; category, starting with the &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/react-form-components-guide" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;React form components guide&lt;/a&gt; for the filter forms around your tables.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Components in this post
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Data Table&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): The admin table on TanStack Table v9: search, filters, sorting, bulk actions, pinning, resizing, virtualization and cards on phones. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add data-table&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Table&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): The library's one table look: semantic table parts plus a props API for small read-only tables that turn into cards on phones. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add table&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/pagination" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Pagination&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): Pages for tables and lists: numbers with ellipses, previous / next, page size, jump to page, a compact phone pager and load more. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add pagination&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/components/toolbar" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Toolbar&lt;/a&gt; (Pro): The bar above a list or table: search, filter chips, applied filters, sort, a list / grid switch, the count, actions and a bulk mode. Install: &lt;code&gt;npx wingo-ui@latest add toolbar&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  FAQ
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  What replaced useReactTable in TanStack Table v9?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;useTable, which takes a required features option built with tableFeatures(). Row models such as sorting and pagination moved into that features object as factories (sortedRowModel: createSortedRowModel()), and useLegacyTable from @tanstack/react-table/legacy keeps the v8 API as a deprecated migration aid.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How do I do server-side pagination in TanStack Table v9?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Set manualPagination: true, pass the total as rowCount, control the pagination state and fetch the page your API returns for it. Add manualSorting and manualFiltering when the server also sorts and filters, and leave the matching row model factories out of tableFeatures, since the browser never runs them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  How do I pin columns in TanStack Table v9?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Register columnPinningFeature and set columnPinning to { start: [...], end: [...] }; v9 renamed left and right to start and end with no aliases. Register columnSizingFeature too, make the pinned cells position: sticky with column.getStart('start') or column.getAfter('end') as the offset, and give them a width and a background so the offsets line up and rows do not show through.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Does the shadcn data table work on phones?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;As of October 2026 the shadcn data table guide covers sorting, filtering, column visibility, client pagination and row selection on TanStack Table v9, and has no phone layout: the table keeps its columns and scrolls sideways. A card per row below 768px is something you add yourself or get from a component that ships it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Is the Wingo UI Data Table free?
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;No. The Data Table is part of Wingo UI Pro, and so are the Table, Pagination and Toolbar it builds on. The checkbox, dropdown menu, popover and other basics it uses are free on their own.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This article was originally published on the Wingo UI blog: &lt;a href="https://wingo-ui.com/blog/tanstack-table-v9-data-table" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;TanStack Table v9 Data Table: Filters, Bulk Actions, Mobile&lt;/a&gt;. The original has the live, interactive demos and is kept up to date.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In the vast landscape of React development, developers are armed with powerful tools to navigate challenges effortlessly: the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; hooks. This guide offers a deep dive into these hooks, unveiling their functionalities, adaptable use cases, and advanced methodologies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What is &lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; hook in React is a cornerstone for managing state, offering a more nuanced approach than its counterpart, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Its value shines brightest when grappling with intricate state logic, especially in scenarios where the state comprises multiple sub-values or the next state hinges on its predecessor.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Basic Usage
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's start with a basic example. Suppose we have a counter component. Instead of resorting to &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, developers can employ &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; to orchestrate&amp;nbsp;state management within their React applications.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Counter.js
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useReducer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;decrement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;+&lt;/span&gt;
       &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;decrement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;-&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Counter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Within this demonstration, the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;reducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; function operates as a pivotal entity responsible for processing both the current state and an action, ultimately yielding a fresh state contingent upon the nature of the action. Through the utilization of the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; hook, we seamlessly integrate this &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;reducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; function alongside the initial state. This amalgamation furnishes us with access to the current state and a potent &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;dispatch&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; function, empowering us to propagate actions directly to the reducer, thereby orchestrating state transformations with precision and efficiency.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Output
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F9fifizn19mwsnodvzbcb.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F9fifizn19mwsnodvzbcb.gif" alt=" " width="800" height="224"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Advanced Usage
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; can handle more complex state objects. You can use it with objects, arrays, or any other data structure. Additionally, you can combine it with &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useContext&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; for global state management or optimize performance with &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useMemo&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useCallback&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
Now, let's explore an advanced usage scenario of &lt;strong&gt;useReducer&lt;/strong&gt; with a more complex state object:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  AdvancedCounter.js
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// AdvancedCounter.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useReducer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;showText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;switch &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;toggleText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;showText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;showText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;};&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;AdvancedCounter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useReducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;initialState&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
         &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;increment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;Increment&lt;/span&gt;
         &lt;span class="nf"&gt;dispatch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;type&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;toggleText&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;})}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;showText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Hide Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Show Text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;state&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;showText&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;This&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;is&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;dynamic&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AdvancedCounter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In this example, our state object comprises both a counter and a Boolean value to toggle text visibility. The reducer function now handles both incrementing the count and toggling the text display.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Output
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk1w5vks9rifs02gyw6yv.gif" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fk1w5vks9rifs02gyw6yv.gif" alt=" " width="800" height="254"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What is &lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;?
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; hook is another essential hook in React. It is primarily used for accessing and manipulating DOM elements directly. Unlike &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useState&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, changes to a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;ref&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; don't cause a re-render. Such versatility makes it apt for various tasks, ranging from overseeing focus management and instigating imperative animations to seamlessly integrating with third-party DOM libraries.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Basic Usage
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Let's create a simple instance to apprehend how &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; works. Suppose we have a form component that requires focusing on an input field when it mounts.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Form.js
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;Form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;inputRef&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;inputRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;focus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[]);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Submit&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;Form&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;In the illustration provided, &lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt; emerges as the linchpin, facilitating the establishment of a reference to the input element within the component's scope. Subsequently, leveraging the &lt;code&gt;effect&lt;/code&gt; hook, we orchestrate the focus onto the input element upon the component's initial rendering, meticulously ensuring that this operation occurs precisely once through the judicious utilization of an empty dependency array &lt;code&gt;[].&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Output
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fijqrj73pz442fbb5sd5u.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fijqrj73pz442fbb5sd5u.png" alt=" " width="800" height="199"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Advanced Usage
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is not limited to DOM elements, it can also persist mutable values across renders without causing re-renders. This property makes it useful for storing previous values, caching values between renders, or interacting with imperative APIs.&lt;br&gt;
Now, let's discover a complicated utilization scenario where &lt;strong&gt;useRef&lt;/strong&gt; is hired to persist mutable values:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  AdvancedForm.js
&lt;/h4&gt;



&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight jsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// AdvancedForm.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;react&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;AdvancedForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderCount&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useRef&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useEffect&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(()&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;++&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nx"&gt;This&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;component&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;has&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;rendered&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;renderCount&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;current&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;times&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;default&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;AdvancedForm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Within this instance, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is ingeniously harnessed to maintain the tally of component renderings throughout successive re-renders, all the while circumventing the unnecessary triggering of additional renders. By employing this tactic, we ensure the seamless preservation of the render counts across iterations, thus exemplifying the versatile capabilities of the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; hook in React development.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h4&gt;


  Output
&lt;/h4&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fubu62ao2hegk37i6jaj1.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2Fubu62ao2hegk37i6jaj1.png" alt=" " width="800" height="201"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  Conclusion
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this guide, we've explored two effective hooks supplied by React: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useReducer&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; is a versatile tool for dealing with complicated state logic, while &lt;strong&gt;&lt;code&gt;useRef&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; gives direct get entry to DOM elements and permits for staying power of mutable values. Understanding and mastering those hooks will notably enhance your React development skills, allowing you to construct greater green and robust packages. Start integrating them into your projects and explore their full potential!&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;CogniPrep's dashboard is a React Server Component that asks a lot of questions about one user: their plan, their streak, their personal best, their recent sessions, which games they have played. Those questions are answered by &lt;code&gt;lib/db/cache.ts&lt;/code&gt;, 219 lines holding nine functions, each wrapped in React's &lt;code&gt;cache()&lt;/code&gt;, imported by ten files.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The file is worth reading not because per-request memoisation is clever, but because almost everything that made it faster had nothing to do with memoisation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The dedupe that quietly did not dedupe
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;cache()&lt;/code&gt; memoises a function for the lifetime of a render pass. The subtlety is in the word &lt;em&gt;function&lt;/em&gt;. The memo is keyed on the wrapped function reference plus its arguments, so two different wrappers that happen to run the same SQL are two separate memos. Which is exactly what we had, documented in &lt;code&gt;lib/plan/plan.ts&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
 * Get user's full data including plan info.
 *
 * Delegates to `getCachedUser` rather than issuing its own query. Both read the
 * same row by primary key, but as separate `cache()` functions they had separate
 * cache entries, so a dashboard render that called `getUserPlan` AND
 * `getCachedUser` (via preloadDashboardData) fetched the identical row twice.
 * Routing every id-keyed read through one function makes the dedupe actually work.
 */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;getUserData&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getCachedUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`User not found: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;${&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Both functions were "cached". Both were correct. Together they ran &lt;code&gt;select * from users where id = $1&lt;/code&gt; twice on every dashboard render, and nothing about either call site looked wrong. The fix is a delegation, not a configuration: one function owns the id-keyed read and everything else goes through it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That funnel is now six paths deep on a single dashboard request. The layout calls &lt;code&gt;getCachedUser&lt;/code&gt;. The page calls it directly, and again through &lt;code&gt;getUserPlan&lt;/code&gt;, and again through &lt;code&gt;getCachedUserStats&lt;/code&gt;, and again through the onboarding insights module, and the preload helper fires it before any of them render. Six reaches, one query, and the only reason it holds is that none of those five callers was allowed to write its own &lt;code&gt;select&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  A wider query, reused, beats a narrower one
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is the comment I would not have predicted writing:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;getCachedUserStats&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Reuses the cached full-row read instead of issuing a second, narrower query&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// for columns that live on the row we have already fetched. The dashboard calls&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// this alongside getUserPlan, so this removes one round trip per render.&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;row&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getCachedUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The streak panel needs five columns. The "efficient" version of that is a five-column select. The actually faster version is to reuse the full row somebody else in this render already paid for, because the cost here is the round trip, not the width of the row. Narrowing a query is a win when it is the only query. It is a loss when it turns one round trip into two.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Half the file's wins are not caching
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The three best commits in this file are all deletions.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dropping an aggregate nobody read:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
 * Returns the rows only. It used to also run a `$count` over the user's entire
 * session history alongside them, to report `total` and `hasMore` - and nothing
 * has ever read either. The dashboard, its only caller, destructures
 * `{ sessions }`. That made every dashboard render pay for a full aggregate over
 * the app's largest table to compute two numbers it discarded.
 */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Dropping a column:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
 * Everything except `metrics`, deliberately. `metrics` is by far the largest
 * column on the most-written table in the app, and this read exists to answer
 * "which games has this user played lately", never "how did that attempt go".
 */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And dropping a second implementation:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;getCachedLatestScores&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;cache&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;async &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;gameIds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[])&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Delegates to the repository rather than reimplementing the query. These two&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// had drifted into duplicate copies of the same unbounded scan, so a fix&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// applied to one silently missed the other.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;None of those are cache behaviour. A memo would have hidden all three: the aggregate still runs once, the jsonb blob still crosses the wire once, the unbounded scan still scans once. Memoisation makes repeated waste cheap, which is a good way to stop noticing waste.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One more, where the ORM's default is the trap:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// The filter is passed into $count so the total is scoped to this user.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Without it Drizzle emits an unfiltered `count(*)` over the whole table.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;logs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;total&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Promise&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;all&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;select&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;auditLogsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;where&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;eq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;auditLogsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;))&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;orderBy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;desc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;auditLogsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;created_at&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;),&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;db&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;$count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;auditLogsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;eq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;auditLogsTable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;user_id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;]);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;A paginated list whose total is the table's total is wrong and fast-looking until the table is large, at which point it is wrong and slow.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The preload pattern only works because of the memo
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two pages start their data before they render anything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;preloadDashboardData&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Fire all queries in parallel - don't await&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getCachedUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getCachedUserStats&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;void&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;getCachedUserSessions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;offset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;void&lt;/code&gt; a cached call at the top of the page, and the components that await it later get the in-flight promise rather than a new query. It is the cheapest waterfall fix in the App Router: no props to thread, no context, no change to the components. It also only works inside the memo's scope, which brings me to the last finding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Two route handlers import a memo that has nowhere to live
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Ten files import this module and two of them are route handlers: the support tickets route pulls &lt;code&gt;getCachedUser&lt;/code&gt;, the audit log route pulls &lt;code&gt;getCachedAuditLogsWithPagination&lt;/code&gt;. A route handler is not a render, so there is no render pass for &lt;code&gt;cache()&lt;/code&gt; to attach its memo to. Nothing is lost today, because each of them calls its function exactly once, and a memo you consult once is just a function call. But the name is making a promise the context cannot keep, and the next person to add a second caller inside that handler will believe the second call is free.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That is the shape of this whole file. Nine functions whose value is mostly in the queries they refuse to run, wrapped in a mechanism that only pays out when two callers in one render ask the same question, and that stops paying out the moment someone writes the same query under a different name.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  See it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This surface is behind the login, so there is no URL to curl. &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Sign up free at cogniprep.app&lt;/a&gt; and load the dashboard: the plan badge, the streak, the personal best and the recent sessions list are all served by one round of these nine functions, and the row behind four of those panels is fetched once. &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The pricing page&lt;/a&gt; sets out what the free tier includes, and the &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/games" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;practice catalogue&lt;/a&gt; is the public half of the same data.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Scroll to the bottom of &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/guides/how-to-host-a-pub-quiz" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;how to host a pub quiz&lt;/a&gt; and you get a short closing section: a heading, one sentence, one button. Do the same on &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/tools/round-planner" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the round planner&lt;/a&gt; and on &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/solutions/pubs" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;quiz night for pubs&lt;/a&gt;. The button says the same eight words all three times. The heading above it is different every time.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;p &lt;span class="k"&gt;in &lt;/span&gt;guides/how-to-host-a-pub-quiz tools/round-planner solutions/pubs&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do
    &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://pub-trivia.app/&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$p&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="se"&gt;\&lt;/span&gt;
      | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Set your first one up\|Run the order you just planned\|Run your first quiz night free'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-u&lt;/span&gt;
Run the order you just planned
Run your first quiz night free
Set your first one up
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That block comes from one component, and the component carries a default for pages that do not supply their own. No page does not supply its own.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The component
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sixty-seven pages render inside one shell. It owns the header, the breadcrumb up to the hub, the related-links block and the footer, so that a page cannot forget any of them, and it owns the closing call to action as well:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;export&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;ContentPage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;width&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;prose&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;article&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;children&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;React&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;ReactNode&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="cm"&gt;/** The single contextual call to action. Defaults to the free trial. */&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;heading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;...&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;})&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;node&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;requireNode&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;action&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;cta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nl"&gt;heading&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Ready for quiz night?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;body&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Set up your venue, print the QR codes, and you're live tonight.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;label&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;Start free, no card needed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="nx"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;/signup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;An optional prop with a &lt;code&gt;??&lt;/code&gt; behind it. The reading is obvious: most pages get the generic trial pitch, and the few with something sharper to say override it. That is how I wrote it, and it is wrong about its own codebase.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"ContentPage"&lt;/span&gt; app &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--include&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"page.tsx"&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
67
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"cta={"&lt;/span&gt; app &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--include&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"page.tsx"&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
67
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Sixty-seven of 67. So those four lines of copy have never rendered, never been read in context, and never been seen by anybody outside the repository. That is checkable from outside, because the heading is a string nothing else on a content page would produce:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;for &lt;/span&gt;u &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; /pricing /faq /features /guides /compare /tools /solutions /quiz-questions&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do
    &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://pub-trivia.app&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$u&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Ready for quiz night'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-u&lt;/span&gt;
0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;With one exception, which is the interesting part:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://pub-trivia.app/ | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'Ready for quiz night'&lt;/span&gt;
1
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The homepage says it. The homepage is not a content page and does not use the component, it has its own closing section, and all four of the default's fields are that section copied across: same heading, same sentence, same button label, same destination. The "default" is not a neutral fallback at all. It is the landing page's closing pitch, pasted into a component the landing page does not render, where it waits for a page that never arrives.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the 67 pages actually say
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I pulled the four fields out of every page and counted the distinct values.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Distinct values across 67 pages&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;heading&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;67&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;body&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;67&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;label&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;href&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sixty-seven headings, no two alike. Sixty-seven bodies, no two alike, between 82 and 172 characters, averaging 124. One button label on all of them, &lt;code&gt;Start free, no card needed&lt;/code&gt;. One destination on all of them, &lt;code&gt;/signup&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The headings are instructions more than questions. Thirteen begin with "Run", nine with "Try", six with "Build". Exactly one ends in a question mark, on &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/faq" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the FAQ&lt;/a&gt;, where "Still deciding?" is a fair description of the reader. The pattern nobody planned is that the heading finishes the page's own argument: the &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/features/question-timer" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;question timer page&lt;/a&gt; closes with "Feel the pacing", the &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/compare/kahoot-for-pub-quiz" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Kahoot comparison&lt;/a&gt; with "Built for a table of four", the &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/quiz-questions/music" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;music questions page&lt;/a&gt; with "Build a music round".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The split between what varies and what does not is the part I would defend. Heading and body are about the page, so they move with the page. Label and destination are about the offer, and the offer does not change because somebody arrived through a comparison table rather than a guide. A button whose wording shifts from page to page teaches a returning visitor nothing. Fifty-three of the 67 bodies mention the trial anyway, in the page's own words, which is where that variation belongs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The site does not hold that line everywhere. The homepage has six links to &lt;code&gt;/signup&lt;/code&gt; with four different labels on them:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://pub-trivia.app/ | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'href="/signup"&amp;gt;[^&amp;lt;]*'&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;sort&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;uniq&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-c&lt;/span&gt;
   1 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/signup"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Get started
   1 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/signup"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Sign up
   1 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/signup"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Start free, no card needed
   3 &lt;span class="nv"&gt;href&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;"/signup"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;Start free trial
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Header, footer, hero, two plan cards and the closing block. Five labels in total across the site for one destination. The 67 pages are the disciplined part by accident: they agree because the label sits in each page's props and got copied, not because anything enforces it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What to do with a default that never fires
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three options, and I do not think the choice is obvious.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep it.&lt;/strong&gt; It costs four lines, and page 68 gets something reasonable if its author forgets. That is the case for defaults in general, and it is why I wrote it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Delete it and make &lt;code&gt;cta&lt;/code&gt; required.&lt;/strong&gt; The type loses its &lt;code&gt;?&lt;/code&gt;, and the compiler asks page 68 the question instead. This is what the code already believes: 67 out of 67 is not a majority, it is a rule. The closing line is the highest-value piece of copy on a marketing page, and "whatever the component says" should not be a decision anybody can make by omission.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Keep it, but not that copy.&lt;/strong&gt; If the fallback is a safety net, it should say the thing that is true on any page, not the thing that works on the landing page. "Ready for quiz night?" assumes the reader came in through a page about running a quiz. Somebody who landed on the scoring systems guide from a search for scoring systems is not ready for anything yet, and that is the one case the net is for.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My own preference is the second, because the default's real status is that of a commented-out block: it sits in the repository, it reads as though it runs, and the first time anybody sees it in context would be on a live page. It is not changed yet, so for now the repository still carries a fifth copy of the homepage's closing pitch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a smaller lesson underneath. Writing &lt;code&gt;cta?:&lt;/code&gt; with a &lt;code&gt;??&lt;/code&gt; behind it is a claim about how often the fallback gets used, and I never checked the claim. One &lt;code&gt;grep -rl&lt;/code&gt; settled it in a second. Any optional prop with a fixed, countable set of call sites can be audited the same way, and the answer is either "this default is load bearing" or "this default is fiction".&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;To see the 67 of them, the hubs are the quickest route: &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/guides" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;guides&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/features" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;features&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/solutions" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;solutions&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/compare" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;comparisons&lt;/a&gt; and the &lt;a href="https://pub-trivia.app/tools" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;free tools&lt;/a&gt;. Bottom of every page, same button, different sentence above it.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Transient feedback in CogniPrep goes through sonner, and there is a small module whose job is to be the only caller:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="cm"&gt;/**
 * App-wide toast helpers.
 *
 * These wrap sonner so transient action feedback (API failures, successful
 * mutations, milestones) is shown as a toast rather than inline UI. Page-level
 * failures should continue to use the route `error.tsx` boundaries instead.
 */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;It exports two functions. &lt;code&gt;notifySuccess(title, description?)&lt;/code&gt; is thin. &lt;code&gt;notifyError(error, options?)&lt;/code&gt; is the one that earns its place:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight typescript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;toErrorMessage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;unknown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Something went wrong. Please try again.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;):&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kr"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fetchJson throws `HTTP 500: &amp;lt;body&amp;gt;` style messages; keep them readable but&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// strip the noisy status prefix when there is a message after it.&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;/^HTTP &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\d&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\s&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;([\s\S]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sr"&gt;$/&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;match&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;??&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;200&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;typeof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;amp;&amp;amp;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;().&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;length&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;trim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Three decisions in nine lines: strip the status prefix our fetch wrapper adds, refuse anything over 200 characters, and accept &lt;code&gt;unknown&lt;/code&gt; so a &lt;code&gt;catch&lt;/code&gt; block can hand it whatever it caught. That is a policy, and it is the right one.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then there is the other path.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"notifyError(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\|&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;notifySuccess("&lt;/span&gt; app components lib | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-v&lt;/span&gt; lib/utils/toast.ts | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
65
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\.\(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\|&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;success&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;("&lt;/span&gt; app components lib | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-v&lt;/span&gt; lib/utils/toast.ts | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
36
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Sixty-five calls through the helper across 18 files, and 36 calls straight to sonner across 15 more. Every destructive dialog in the settings area, both auth forms, the support ticket forms, the help page contact form, two report pages and the interview download button import &lt;code&gt;toast&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;sonner&lt;/code&gt; and do their own formatting.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  It is not simply legacy code
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The helper landed on 13 August 2026, in a commit called "Add game milestones, provider persistence, and toast notifications". Most of the direct callers are older than that: the delete-session dialog dates to 3 April, the login form and the account deletion dialog to 11 May. A helper arriving after its call sites is the ordinary way a codebase ends up with two of something.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What is not ordinary is this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;git log &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--diff-filter&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;A &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--format&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'%ad'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;short &lt;span class="nt"&gt;--&lt;/span&gt; components/support/SupportChat.tsx
2026-08-28
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The support chat and the ticket reply form were created on 28 August, a fortnight after the helper, and they account for 8 of the 36. New code picked up the old habit, because the old habit is what the neighbouring files do. A convention that is not enforced is a suggestion, and a suggestion loses to copy and paste every time.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the difference actually costs
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the account deletion dialog:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Deletion failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;description&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;instanceof&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;?&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Failed to delete account&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And here is a settings card doing the same job through the helper:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;notifyError&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;title&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Deletion failed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;fallback&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;Please try again.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;They look identical on screen in the happy failure case. They differ when the error is ugly. The first renders &lt;code&gt;error.message&lt;/code&gt; as it arrives, so an &lt;code&gt;HTTP 500: {"error":"..."}&lt;/code&gt; string from the fetch wrapper goes on screen with the status prefix intact, and a 2 KB message goes on screen in full. The second strips the prefix and, past 200 characters, replaces the whole thing with a sentence a human wrote. The first path is also where a server-action string lands directly in the UI, in both auth forms:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;await&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loginUser&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;''&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;},&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;formDataObj&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;?.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="nx"&gt;toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;result&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;message&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That works because the server actions are careful about what they put in &lt;code&gt;message&lt;/code&gt;. It works by agreement, not by construction.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Four copies of the same download
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While counting, the duplicate literals fell out:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;   4 'PDF downloaded'
   4 'Failed to download PDF. Please try again.'
   4 'Deletion failed'
   2 'Please fix the errors before submitting'
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The PDF pair appears in four files: two report pages, the interview download button and the exercise results screen. Each one is the same shape, a fetch, a blob, an anchor click, a success toast, a failure toast. Four copies of a block whose only variable is the URL. The toast strings are not the problem there, they are the fingerprint of the problem, which is that nobody extracted &lt;code&gt;downloadPdf()&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The part the audit made me look at
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Sonner's container is mounted once, in the root layout, with one option:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nc"&gt;Toaster&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;position&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"top-right"&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;No &lt;code&gt;duration&lt;/code&gt;, no &lt;code&gt;closeButton&lt;/code&gt;, no &lt;code&gt;richColors&lt;/code&gt;. So every toast in the product is a default-duration toast, roughly four seconds, with no way to hold it open. On every public page there is an empty live region waiting for it, which you can see without triggering anything:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;Object&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;fromEntries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;([...&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;document&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;querySelector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;section[aria-live]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;attributes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;].&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;map&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;a&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]))&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// {&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//   "aria-label": "Notifications alt+T",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//   "tabindex": "-1",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//   "aria-live": "polite",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//   "aria-relevant": "additions text",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;//   "aria-atomic": "false"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// }&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;polite&lt;/code&gt; for everything, which is sonner's design and is right for "Session deleted" and arguable for "your account was not deleted". A polite announcement waits for the screen reader to finish whatever it is saying, by which point a four-second toast may be gone. Several of our destructive dialogs use that toast as the only report that the destruction did not happen.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One thing is already handled, and it is the only place in this codebase where a library's motion got a second thought:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-sonner-toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-mounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;'true'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-sonner-toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-removed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;'true'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That works because sonner animates with CSS, which the stylesheet can reach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The call
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two paths is the finding; picking one is the work. The helper wins on merit, so the 36 direct calls want migrating and the lint rule that forbids importing &lt;code&gt;toast&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;code&gt;sonner&lt;/code&gt; outside &lt;code&gt;lib/utils/toast.ts&lt;/code&gt; is what stops the count going back up. The four download blocks want one function. And the failures that must not be missed, deleting an account, losing a reply, want something that outlives four seconds, because a toast is a notification, not a record.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  See it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/help" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cogniprep.app/help&lt;/a&gt; and run that attributes line in the console. You get sonner's live region, empty, with its &lt;code&gt;alt+T&lt;/code&gt; shortcut, on a page where no toast has fired yet. The contact form on that page is a real support form, so please do not submit one just to watch an animation. &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/games" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;The practice catalogue&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/pricing" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the pricing page&lt;/a&gt; carry the same empty region, because it is mounted in the root layout for all 375 routes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Someone had been careful with motion preferences in CogniPrep's stylesheet. There are five &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt; blocks in &lt;code&gt;app/globals.css&lt;/code&gt;, and they are not the one-line kind you paste in from a blog post.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is the global brake:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;/* Respect user's motion preferences */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;*,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;::before&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nd"&gt;::after&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation-duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.01ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation-iteration-count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;transition-duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.01ms&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nl"&gt;scroll-behavior&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;auto&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.stars-layer&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-sonner-toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-mounted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;'true'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;],&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="o"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-sonner-toast&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;data-removed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;'true'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;none&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="cp"&gt;!important&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;There is a block that makes sure the fade-up elements, which start at &lt;code&gt;opacity: 0&lt;/code&gt; so they do not shift layout, are forced visible rather than left invisible forever. There are two &lt;code&gt;no-preference&lt;/code&gt; blocks, so the &lt;code&gt;will-change&lt;/code&gt; hints and the scroll-timeline reveal are only set up for people who want motion:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;@supports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;animation-timeline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;())&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;no-preference&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nc"&gt;.reveal-on-scroll&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="nl"&gt;animation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reveal-rise&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;linear&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;both&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="py"&gt;animation-timeline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;view&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;();&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="py"&gt;animation-range&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;70%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And my favourite, which is the opposite of switching an animation off. One practice exercise has an element that shakes just before it bursts, and the shake is the only warning. Deleting the motion deletes the information, so the reduced version redefines the keyframe to pulse the opacity instead:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight css"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;/* Shaking is the only cue that a bubble is about to burst, so keep a reduced
   version rather than removing the motion entirely. */&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;@media&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;reduce&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;@keyframes&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;numbubble-shake&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="m"&gt;0%&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;100%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="m"&gt;50%&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nl"&gt;opacity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;0.55&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That is someone thinking about the actual user. So I turned the preference on, loaded the home page, and watched three dots bounce up and down forever.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  None of it reaches framer-motion
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Thirteen files in the repository import &lt;code&gt;framer-motion&lt;/code&gt;. Zero import &lt;code&gt;useReducedMotion&lt;/code&gt;. Zero use &lt;code&gt;MotionConfig&lt;/code&gt;. The grep is the whole story:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rl&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"framer-motion"&lt;/span&gt; app components | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
13
&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-rn&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"useReducedMotion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="se"&gt;\|&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;MotionConfig"&lt;/span&gt; app components lib | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
0
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The reason that matters is the library's default, which surprised me more than our own omission. From &lt;code&gt;framer-motion@12.42.2&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// dist/es/context/MotionConfigContext.mjs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;MotionConfigContext&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;createContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;({&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;transformPagePoint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;isStatic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="na"&gt;reducedMotion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;never&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;});&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And the resolution:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducedMotion&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;useContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;MotionConfigContext&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducedMotion&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;never&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;else&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducedMotion&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;===&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s2"&gt;always&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;reducedMotionPreference&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;"never"&lt;/code&gt; means never reduce. The library ships the media query listener and then, by default, declines to consult it. You opt in with &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MotionConfig reducedMotion="user"&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; or by branching on &lt;code&gt;useReducedMotion()&lt;/code&gt; yourself. We do neither, so every &lt;code&gt;animate&lt;/code&gt; prop in the codebase runs at full amplitude no matter what the operating system says.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The global CSS brake cannot save it either, and this is the part worth internalising. That &lt;code&gt;*&lt;/code&gt; rule governs &lt;code&gt;animation-duration&lt;/code&gt; and &lt;code&gt;transition-duration&lt;/code&gt;, which are CSS properties. A JavaScript animation library writes the value itself, frame by frame, into the inline style. There is no duration declaration for the stylesheet to override, so the &lt;code&gt;!important&lt;/code&gt; lands on nothing.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What is actually still moving
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four animations in the repository repeat forever, and two of them are on the public home page. The typing indicator in the interview demo:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight tsx"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;motion&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nt"&gt;div&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;className&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s"&gt;"h-2 w-2 rounded-full bg-white/70"&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;animate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;y&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;transition&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;duration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.55&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;repeat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;Infinity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;delay&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nx"&gt;i&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mf"&gt;0.15&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;ease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;easeInOut&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="dl"&gt;'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="si"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;/&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;And a recording dot at &lt;code&gt;duration: 1.2&lt;/code&gt;, plus a dash offset at &lt;code&gt;1.5&lt;/code&gt; and a blurred halo scaling at &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt; in the report demo. Infinite, translating, and completely unaware of the preference. For a product whose users are mostly about to sit a timed assessment, "I asked the system to calm down and the marketing page is still breathing at me" is not a good first impression.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Counting is useful here: the two biggest offenders by motion density are the feedback report demo (52 motion elements and props) and the interview demo (39). A per-component &lt;code&gt;useReducedMotion()&lt;/code&gt; audit of those two files is most of the work.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  While I was in the chunks
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I checked whether the library even reaches the home page, because it is loaded through five &lt;code&gt;next/dynamic&lt;/code&gt; calls that are commented "Lazy-load heavy components". The initial HTML has 18 async &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;script src&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; tags, and one of them is 135,739 bytes and contains framer-motion:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="nv"&gt;$ &lt;/span&gt;curl &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-s&lt;/span&gt; https://cogniprep.app/ | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;grep&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-o&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'src="/_next/static/chunks/[^"]*"'&lt;/span&gt; | &lt;span class="nb"&gt;wc&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nt"&gt;-l&lt;/span&gt;
18
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Pull that chunk and grep it for &lt;code&gt;beforeChildren&lt;/code&gt;, a string only framer-motion has a use for, and it is there. So the dynamic import defers the demo components, not the animation runtime: 132 KB of it is in the initial download either way. The same chunk also contains the literal &lt;code&gt;prefers-reduced-motion&lt;/code&gt;, which is the listener the default configuration never asks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;There is a note in one landing section about exactly this cost:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt; * This used to be a Client Component whose only reason to be one was two
 * framer-motion `whileInView` fade-ups. That cost the landing page, the most
 * requested and most SEO-critical route in the app, twice over: the animation
 * library itself (~132 KB) in the initial bundle, and `lib/games/constants`
 * (~4,400 lines of game and provider metadata) shipped to the browser purely so
 * this markup could be rendered there instead of on the server.
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;That section was converted to a Server Component and its reveal is now the CSS scroll timeline above, which is why the CSS half of this story is in such good shape. The JavaScript half was never revisited.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The fix, in order of value
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;One &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;MotionConfig reducedMotion="user"&amp;gt;&lt;/code&gt; around the client tree fixes the default for everything in one line, and framer-motion's own behaviour under it is sensible: it drops transform and layout animations and keeps opacity, which is the same judgement the hand-written keyframe above makes. Then the four infinite loops want a deliberate decision each, because &lt;code&gt;repeat: Infinity&lt;/code&gt; is the one thing people with vestibular disorders report as actively unpleasant. None of that is deployed yet; this post is the audit, not the patch.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  See it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Open DevTools on &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cogniprep.app&lt;/a&gt;, go to the Rendering panel, and set "Emulate CSS media feature prefers-reduced-motion" to &lt;code&gt;reduce&lt;/code&gt;. Reload. The scroll reveals stop, the toast animations stop, and the demo panels further down the page keep animating exactly as before. Then try the same on &lt;a href="https://cogniprep.app/games" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;the practice catalogue&lt;/a&gt;, which is server-rendered and has no JavaScript animation on it at all: there, the setting does everything you would expect.&lt;/p&gt;
